!!! theorem "Theorem 123.14 : Conjugate-Linearity in the First Argument"
    Any inner product $(\cdot,\cdot)$ is conjugate-linear in the first argument (cf. **Theorem 2.1.14**):

    $$
    \begin{equation*}
    \left(\sum_{i} \lambda_{i}\left|w_{i}\right\rangle,|v\rangle\right)=\sum_{i} \lambda_{i}^{*}\left(\left|w_{i}\right\rangle,|v\rangle\right) . \tag{2.15}
    \end{equation*}
    $$

!!! example "Example 123.15: Inner Product on $\mathbb{C}^{n}$"
    The vector space $\mathbb{C}^{n}$ has an inner product defined by

    $$
    \left(\left(y_{1}, \ldots, y_{n}\right),\left(z_{1}, \ldots, z_{n}\right)\right) \equiv \sum_{i} y_{i}^{*} z_{i}
    =\left[y_{1}^{*} \ldots y_{n}^{*}\right]\left[\begin{array}{c}
    z_{1} \\
    \vdots \\
    z_{n}
    \end{array}\right].
    $$

